147:The EEG preprocessing and artifact removal pipeline is executed through a multi-stage, fully automated algorithm. Initially, EEG signals undergo bandpass /uniFB01 ltering between 1 and 100 Hz, focusing on the relevant frequency range for subsequent analysis. Signals were downsampled to 200 Hz. Bad EEG channels are identi /uniFB01 ed using the pyprep algorithm, which employs a RANSAC -based approach, and these channels are subsequently interpolated using information from neighboring electrodes [47, 48]. RANSAC works by randomly selecting a small group of EEG channels, estimating a model based on these channels, and then identifying channels that deviate from the model as potential outliers or bad channels. This process is repeated to /uniFB01 nd a model that best /uniFB01 ts the majority of channels while disregarding outliers. Then, re-referencing is performed using the common average reference method to minimize common noise across electrodes. Independent Component Analysis (ICA) is then applied, and the IClabel algorithm automatically identi /uniFB01 es and rejects components related to eye blinks [49]. A second bandpass /uniFB01 lter narrows the frequency range to 1 -45 Hz, re /uniFB01 ning the data further. The EEG signals are segmented into 10-second epochs based on experimental paradigms (e.g., eyes-open and eyes-closed). The Autoreject toolbox [50] is utilized for the detection and cleaning/rejection of bad epochs, ensuring the removal of artifacts or irregularities. All EEG datasets underwent the preprocessing steps described above, except when certain procedures were deemed infeasible. Notably, the Autoreject step was excluded from the preprocessing of the ABCCT dataset. The femaleASD dataset had already been preprocessed and segmented into 2-second epochs. Therefore, further preprocessing for this dataset was con /uniFB01 ned solely to downsampling and re-referencing.
151:Spectral features . As previously stated, a normative model estimates the relationship between a response variable and one or more covariates. In the context of this study, we are interested in the spectral features of the EEG signal as the designated response variable. This choice was motivated by the large literature about the alterations of EEG power in psychiatric disorders [13]. The power spectrum density (PSD) for each epoch and each channel is computed using Welch ' s method (1-second Hann window with a 50% overlap, and a spectral resolution of 0.5 Hz). PSDs are averaged across all epochs within a single subject. To assess the relative power in speci /uniFB01 c frequency bands (delta [1 -4 Hz], theta [4 -8 Hz], alpha [8 -13 Hz], beta [13 -30 Hz], gamma [30 -45 Hz]), the absolute power within each narrow band is divided by the power within the broader band [1 -45 Hz].
153:Functional connectivity . EEG-based functional networks were computed using the HD-EEG source connectivity method, as described in [35]. Brie /uniFB02 y, cortical sources are computed using the exact low-resolution brain electromagnetic tomography (eLORETA) which aims to reconstruct the cortical activity from EEG data with correct localization [51]. In our case, the noise covariance matrix was set to the identity matrix, and the regularization parameter λ was set to 0.1 (inversely proportional to the signal-to-noise ratio). Age-speci /uniFB01 c head models of the three layers (brain, skull, and scalp) were built using an MRI template of young children (4 -18 y) [52]. We used the Boundary Element Method (BEM) provided by the MNE Python package. The forward and inverse models were solved within a source space of 4098 sources per hemisphere (with a ~ 5 mm spacing between sources). Then, to streamline the complexity of the cortical sources, we downsampled them to 68 representative sources by averaging the sources within each region as de /uniFB01 ned by the DesikanKilliany atlas [53]. Subsequently, we computed functional connectivity between pairwise regions of interest, using the amplitude envelope correlation (AEC) method, de /uniFB01 ned as the Pearson correlation between signals ' envelopes derived from the Hilbert transform [36, 37]. Zero-lag signal overlaps caused by spatial leakage were removed using a pairwise orthogonalization approach before connectivity computation [38].
155:Following preprocessing and feature extraction, the cohort was restricted to participants aged 5 to 18 years. Larger diagnostic groups, including ADHD and ANX were downsampled while ensuring a balanced representation across age, sex, and site/study covariates. The /uniFB01 nal dataset used for analysis is described in detail in the data description section.
181:Residual plots: To assess the performance of our models, as recommended by Stasinopoulos et al. [55] we inspected their Q-Q plots. A good model is characterized by the presence of randomly scattered residuals around the horizontal zero line. Additionally, the kernel density estimate of the residuals should approximately follow a normal distribution, and an ideal Q-Q plot should exhibit linearity [55]. Upon reviewing our models, it appears that they demonstrate satisfactory /uniFB01 t and quality, Fig. S15, S16 in Supplementary Materials.
