   123	## Limitations
   124	
   125	While our sample size is considered signi /uniFB01 cantly large for EEG studies (n ~ 2200), it does not reach the scale often seen in MRI and fMRI studies, nor is it representative of the broader population. Furthermore, the sample size of the datasets was not uniform. The largest dataset, HBN, comprised 1539 subjects out of the total 2234 subjects considered in this study, which could bias the results. Moreover, data for this article has been sourced from /uniFB01 ve studies (detailed in Methods) utilizing 128channel EEG systems. While this somewhat controls for variability stemming from the spatial resolution of the collected EEG data, it also introduces limitations to the generalizability (over other EEG systems) of the results. In addition, our NM results are based on cross-sectional data, capturing individual deviations at a single time point. This limits our ability to assess within-subject variability and developmental trajectories. Incorporating longitudinal data could provide a clearer picture of heterogeneity by distinguishing between stable and transient variations.
   126	
   127	One potential limitation is that the population included in the study has a restricted age range (5 -18 years), encompassing only younger, developmental stages. This may contribute to the observed heterogeneity, potentially due to varying developmental trajectories. However, in our recent study involving healthy controls and patients with neurodegenerative diseases [41], we observed similar patterns of heterogeneity in a population aged over 40. We therefore speculate that, although age may moderate certain features, the observed heterogeneity is not solely attributable to age differences. A more comprehensive understanding could be achieved through lifespan models highlighting developmental and non-developmental phases.
   128	
   129	Another challenge of the study stems from the inherent heterogeneity that may exist within each group of patients who present multiple psychiatric diagnoses. This comorbidity (Table S1) introduces a layer of complexity. The presence of comorbidities poses challenges in isolating the unique contributions of each condition. This complexity is re /uniFB02 ective of the clinical reality where patients frequently exhibit overlapping symptomatology, necessitating a comprehensive approach to diagnosis and treatment. We were aware of this key point when quantifying heterogeneity and we provided an additional control analysis by investigating heterogeneity among patients who have only one diagnosis (according to the HBN dataset). The overlap results of the functional connectivity were always low and did not exceed 30%. This con /uniFB01 rms that the observed heterogeneity is indeed intrinsic to the disorder and not driven by the possible comorbidity. In addition, we have controlled for some available parameters such as IQ (Fig. S12 -S14) and sex (Fig. S2 -S11, Table S2, S3).
   130	
   131	Another limitation of this study is the potential in /uniFB02 uence of medication on EEG results in the assessed psychiatric patient cohort. While our research endeavors to elucidate neurobiological patterns, it is essential to recognize that medication-induced effects may introduce confounding variables. Psychotropic medications commonly prescribed for symptom management could impact the recorded EEG signals, potentially complicating the attribution of observed changes solely to underlying psychiatric conditions. Individual variations and medication interactions remain challenging to fully account for. Future studies may bene /uniFB01 t from more extensive participant pro /uniFB01 ling, including detailed pharmacological histories (when available), to enable a more nuanced analysis of the interplay between medication and EEG outcomes.
   132	
   133	Our choice of the features to model was based on the most dominant features in the literature, however, it is plausible that alternative EEG features not included in our analysis could reveal greater homogeneity among the clinical groups. For instance, studies have highlighted the utility of alpha peak frequency for subtyping in ADHD [42], and it would be interesting to examine heterogeneity in this feature to determine whether NM could enhance the subtyping process. A potential further analysis is to use other EEG features or combine other modalities such as MRI with EEG. Presently, the capability to train normative models for multiple response variables using GAMLSS is unavailable, thereby limiting modeling versatility. This constraint manifests in several ways. Firstly, individual models must be trained for each channel, connection, or region, escalating the complexity of both the procedure and its transition into a clinical tool. Secondly, these models are calibrated to the average values of respective channels, connections, or regions, potentially resulting in /uniFB01 tting inaccuracies for each model. Lastly, and notably, the inclusion of multiple response variables could yield a more rigorous representation of the deviation at the subject level.
   134	
   135	## CONCLUSION
   136	
   137	Our investigation of the electrophysiological heterogeneity across four neurodevelopmental and psychiatric disorders reveals that EEG spectral and connectivity deviations from a normative population are extremely heterogeneous. Our /uniFB01 ndings emphasize the urgency of going beyond the average brain and adopting innovative EEG (and more broadly neuroimaging) approaches at the patient level, steering the /uniFB01 eld toward precision psychiatry. The complex tapestry of individual differences in EEG signatures underscores the inadequacy of current one-size/uniFB01 ts-all approaches. The call for tailored, patient-speci /uniFB01 c interventions becomes more pronounced as we navigate the intricate terrain of psychiatric heterogeneity, ultimately striving for a paradigm shift in the way we approach and understand these complex disorders.
   138	
   139	## METHODS Dataset
