   185	Deviation scores / overlap maps . After selecting and validating the model parameters, we trained GAMLSS models for each channel/connection across all frequency bands for the reference healthy group. Subsequently, we project the test data (i.e., relative power and functional connectivity values), of our clinical groups (ADHD, ASD, ANX, LD) and the held-out healthy control group HC(test), onto the corresponding models. This process enables us to calculate deviation scores, known as z-scores (normalized quantile residuals [56]), for each channel/connection for each subject, resulting in an individual deviation map for each subject. The zscores, or deviation scores, are derived using a modi /uniFB01 ed function from the gamlss library. They are computed as the inverse normal cumulative distribution function (CDF) of the model ' s /uniFB01 tted CDF, a method known as randomized quantile residuals (RQRs). According to Stasinopoulos et al. [55], when the model is properly trained, RQRs are equivalent to z-scores. An extreme deviation is de /uniFB01 ned as |z-score| &gt; 2. Consequently, we derived positive and negative extreme deviation maps for z-scores &gt; 2 and &lt; -2, respectively. We then computed the number of subjects exhibiting at least one extreme deviation, as well as the number of extreme deviations per subject. Additionally, for each channel/connection, we assessed the percentage of subjects exhibiting extreme deviation at that location among those with at least one extreme deviation, leading to an overlap map of the extreme deviation in each group.
   191	## DATA AVAILABILITY
   195	## CODE AVAILABILITY
   197	Codes are available at https://github.com/MINDIG-1/NM-Psy.git. For statistical modeling, we employed the gamlss package in R [57]. EEG signal processing is done using the MNE-python package (https://mne.tools/stable/index.html). Networks are visualized using BrainNet Viewer [58] (https://www.nitrc.org/projects/bnv/). Network comparisons were performed utilizing the network-based statistic (NBS) tool [39] (https://www.nitrc.org/projects/nbs).
