   189	Network-based statistical comparison and clinical associations of deviation scores . Deviation scores obtained from these models can also be used as inputs for downstream analyses. Instead of using the raw features (i.e. relative power, functional connectivity, etc.), group average, classi /uniFB01 cation, and prediction analyses can be run using the deviation scores inferred from the models that can serve as inputs [26]. To test this, we have explored whether the metrics derived from NM could outperform classical features in differentiating between healthy controls and patient groups. Using a network-based statistics -NBS- approach (permutation test, n = 5000) [39], we compared the clinical groups to the HC(test) using either the original functional connectivity matrices (the original features) or the deviation score (z-scores) matrices. Second, we explored the potential of NM to provide a framework for generating subject-speci /uniFB01 c markers that may correlate with clinical assessments of patients. For this purpose, we calculated a global deviation score for each subject, de /uniFB01 ned as the average z-score values of the extremely deviated channels/connections. This score was then correlated with the patients ' clinical assessments (total ADOS scores -a standard clinical assessment in ASD-).
